.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt HALFINIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm halfinit
.Nd Lua userspace service manager and shadow startup planner
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl config Ar file
.Op Fl Fl target Ar name
.Op Fl Fl runtime-dir Ar directory
.Op Fl Fl state-file Ar file
.Op Fl Fl run-for Ar seconds
.Op Fl Fl check | Fl Fl graph | Fl Fl dry-run | Fl Fl shadow
.Op Fl Fl simulate Op Fl Fl scenario Ar name
.Op Fl Fl module-dir Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
loads declarative Lua services, validates their graph and supervises foreground
children using HalfBSD process descriptors and kqueue.
It runs as an ordinary userspace process. Installing it does not replace init,
rc or the active boot sequence and does not enable any service automatically.
.Pp
Without an execution mode or explicit configuration,
.Nm
rejects startup. The default system graph is for planning only.
.Fl Fl check , Fl Fl graph
and
.Fl Fl dry-run
report validation, parallel startup waves, reverse shutdown waves and missing
prerequisites as JSON without executing tasks or creating runtime files.
.Fl Fl shadow
also reads bounded rc.conf inputs for a comparison of known enable flags.
It does not execute rc configuration or adopt existing processes.
.Pp
.Fl Fl simulate
uses disposable Lua fixtures in the actual scheduler and supervisor.
Scenarios are success, failure, recovery, exhaustion and forced.
Fixtures have independent lifetime bounds.
Select a private runtime directory when running unprivileged.
.Fl Fl run-for
sets an optional manager lifetime bound (at most 3600 seconds) for isolated tests;
normal managers remain persistent until shutdown.
.Pp
An explicit configuration file returns a table with services, target and optional
include (definition files), service_dirs, runtime_dir, state_file, log_file,
max_parallel and shutdown_timeout fields.
System definitions are under
.Pa /usr/lib/halfbsd/services ;
administrator files belong under
.Pa /etc/halfbsd
or an explicitly supplied private path.
No administrator configuration is overwritten by installworld.
.Pp
The runtime directory must be owned by the manager UID and not group/world
writable. An advisory lock prevents a second manager using the same directory.
The control socket is
.Pa control.sock ;
enabled preferences are written atomically to
.Pa enabled.json
unless an alternate state file is supplied.
.Sh SERVICES
Services have a name, description, type, requires, wants, after, before and
optional process/credential/readiness/restart attributes.
Definitions load in a restricted declarative environment without OS, process,
IO or module-loading operations.
.Bl -tag -width oneshot
.It Sy daemon
A foreground process, supervised until stopped or failed.
.It Sy oneshot
A command becoming ready after exit zero, or an explicit native action provider.
.It Sy target
A group without its own process.
.It Sy virtual
A bounded registered condition provider, without its own process.
.El
.Pp
Requires activates dependencies and gates on READY. Failure stops active hard
dependents; they can resume after recovery. Wants activates optional dependencies
and waits for them to settle, without propagating their failure.
After/before add ordering only if both services are selected.
All combined dependency/ordering cycles are rejected before startup.
Independent eligible services start concurrently, subject to max_parallel.
.Pp
Readiness may be process, notify or condition. Process means successful exec
only and is not proof of daemon initialization. Notify provides a descriptor
number in HALFINIT_READY_FD; the child writes exactly READY followed by newline.
Condition invokes a registered provider through bounded kernel-timer retries.
Start_timeout bounds readiness; stop_timeout bounds TERM grace.
.Pp
Standard input is null. Standard output/error default to null; stdout/stderr
may specify absolute file paths, opened append-only through the native process
API. Manager lifecycle logs remain separate structured records.
.Pp
Restart policies are never, on-failure and always. Restart_limit/window bound
retry bursts. Delay grows exponentially to restart_max_delay. Exhaustion is
terminal until an explicit retry/restart. Readiness failures count as failures.
.Pp
Lifecycle states are INACTIVE, QUEUED, STARTING, READY, STOPPING, FAILED,
RESTARTING and SKIPPED. Every transition records monotonic time, previous/new
state and reason; history is bounded. Logs use halfbsd.log structured records.
.Sh CONTROL AND SHUTDOWN
TERM and INT request reverse dependency shutdown. Independent services within a
wave stop together. TERM grace is followed by KILL and native reaping. An overall
shutdown deadline and final five-second reap bound prevent indefinite waiting.
HUP requests configuration reload.
.Pp
Reload validates the entire candidate before committing it. Active service
removal/type/dependency changes are rejected; stop them first. Other changed
launch attributes apply at the next launch, while restart policy applies to
subsequent failures. Runtime/socket/state/log relocation is rejected.
Changing the initial target during reload is rejected; use explicit target
start/stop commands or restart the manager with the new target.
Invalid candidates preserve the live registry.
.Sh SECURITY
The Unix socket authenticates FreeBSD peer credentials. Read-only commands are
available to clients which can reach it; mutations require manager UID or root.
Requests are versioned JSON, size-bounded and never evaluated as Lua or shell.
Connections and their lifetimes are bounded.
.Pp
System-provided filesystem, networking, firewall, DNS and desktop definitions
are protected planning tasks during this phase. The manager cannot launch a
second instance of an rc-managed service through that graph.
DNS retains the compiled provider hierarchy; DHCP DNS changes are deferred.
.Pp
Foreground execution is required. Escaped/daemonized descendants are not owned
reliably after leader reaping; stale process groups are never signaled.
Desktop session/controlling-terminal ownership needs a future boot integration
adapter. Built-in desktop planning never launches a root compositor.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr halfsvc 8 ,
.Xr lua-halfbsd 7
